# Jittered points: geom_jitter

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_jitter()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_jitter.html), in the order the page
gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot
draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of
what remains.

`geom_jitter()` is a shortcut for `geom_point(position = "jitter")`: it moves each point by a small
random amount, so that points which would sit on one another, as discrete values make them do, can
be told apart. Giggly has no jitter yet, neither `geom_jitter()` nor `position_jitter()`; it draws
the same points unmoved, with `Giggly.Plot.geom_point/3`. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and
drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`,
so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the
cell with `Kino.Image`. The data in these examples is `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel
economy for 234 cars of 38 popular models from 1999 to 2008, which the setup cell binds to `mpg`,
ggplot2's name for it.

## Points without jitter

```r
p <- ggplot(mpg, aes(cyl, hwy))
p + geom_point()
```

Many cars share both a number of cylinders and a highway mileage, so their points are drawn on top
of one another.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :cyl, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> render.()
```

## Jittered points

```r
p + geom_jitter()
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_jitter()` and the position it adds, `position_jitter()`.

## Mapping colour

```r
# Add aesthetic mappings
p + geom_jitter(aes(colour = class))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_jitter()`, and mapping `colour`, the scale that maps it and the legend
that reads it back.

## Less jitter

```r
# Use smaller width/height to emphasise categories
ggplot(mpg, aes(cyl, hwy)) +
  geom_jitter()
ggplot(mpg, aes(cyl, hwy)) +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.25)
```

Unless told otherwise, the jitter moves a point up to 40% of the data's resolution either way;
a smaller `width` keeps each number of cylinders in a narrower column.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_jitter()` and its `width` option.

## More jitter

```r
# Use larger width/height to completely smooth away discreteness
ggplot(mpg, aes(cty, hwy)) +
  geom_jitter()
ggplot(mpg, aes(cty, hwy)) +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.5, height = 0.5)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_jitter()` and its `width` and `height` options.
